# Open decisions — {{PROJECT_NAME}}

Decisions this project has not made yet, each with a **trigger**: the moment it stops
being cheap to leave open.

This is not a to-do list and not a decision log. `DECISIONS.md` (or
`.planning/decisions-archive.md`) records what was decided and why, after the fact. This
file records what has **not** been decided, and makes the gate fail when a trigger fires
while the row is still open.

The point is not to decide early. Nobody knows most of these on day one, and pretending
otherwise produces a guess dressed as a commitment. The point is that you are *asked* at
the moment the answer gets expensive — instead of discovering the question six
deliverables later, when changing your mind costs a subsystem.

## How a row works

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| ID | `D-1`, `D-2`, … Stable; referenced from commits and the contract. |
| Decision | The question, phrased so a one-line answer would close it. |
| Status | `UNDECIDED`, `DECIDED`, or `DEFERRED`. |
| Trigger | One or more globs, `deliverable:N`, or `always`. Comma-separated, backticks optional. |
| Answer | The decision once made, or for `DEFERRED`, a dated reason and a review date. |

`scripts/checks/decisions-due.mjs` fails when a row is `UNDECIDED` and any of its
triggers matches a file that now exists. Closing a row means writing the answer and
setting Status to `DECIDED` — then, if it is architectural, copying it into the contract.

Write triggers **wider than feels necessary**. A trigger that never matches anything is
a decision that will surprise you; the audit reports dead triggers for exactly that
reason. A trigger that fires slightly early costs one conversation.

